<section id="overview">
  <div class="sh"><span class="tile"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M6 3h9l4 4v14H6z"/><path d="M14 3v4h4"/><path d="M9.5 12h6M9.5 16h4"/></svg></span><h2>Overview</h2></div>
  <p>I tested placED. with students through five realistic placement situations. The goal was to see whether students could understand and use the main features without extra explanation.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <tr><th>Area</th><th>Details</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Testing method</td><td>Moderated usability testing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tasks</td><td>5 realistic placement tasks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recorded sessions analysed</td><td>12 recorded screen sessions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What I observed</td><td>Navigation, hesitation, errors, skipped information and user comments</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="groups">
  <div class="sh"><span class="tile"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M16 20v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H7a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="7" r="3.2"/><path d="M17 11h5M19.5 8.5v5"/></svg></span><h2>Who I tested the session with</h2></div>
  <div class="who">
    <div class="r">
      <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="m12 4 10 5-10 5L2 9z"/><path d="M6 11v5c0 1.5 3 3 6 3s6-1.5 6-3v-5"/></svg></span>
      <div><h4>College students - primary audience</h4>
      <p>Final-year students who are applying, or about to. They do all five tasks. The findings from this group decide what changes.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M16 20v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H7a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="7" r="3.2"/><path d="M17 11h5M19.5 8.5v5"/></svg></span>
      <div><h4>Placement cell and faculty - expert review</h4>
      <p>They know the process end to end. They check whether the app matches how drives actually run, not whether it's easy to use.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="posttest">
  <div class="sh"><span class="tile"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M9.2 9.2a2.9 2.9 0 1 1 3.6 3.6c-.6.3-.8.8-.8 1.4"/><path d="M12 17.4h.01"/></svg></span><h2>Post Test questions I asked</h2></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Which part actually worked for you? Which part felt like extra work?</li>
    <li>Would you connect your real inbox? What would have to be true first?</li>
    <li>Should the app change the status on its own, or ask you first?</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="learn">
  <div class="sh"><span class="tile"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8.5"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="1"/></svg></span><h2>What I wanted to learn</h2></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Can users set up placED. and connect their placement email?</li>
    <li>Can users capture a job posting easily?</li>
    <li>Can users choose the right resume and complete an application?</li>
    <li>Do users trust an automatically detected placement update?</li>
    <li>Can users resolve a schedule clash and review the message before it is sent?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px">Timing note: some recordings ended before every task was completed. Where a reliable end time was not available, the timing should be marked as “Not captured” rather than estimated.</p>
</section>

<section id="tasks">
  <div class="sh"><span class="tile"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M8 6h13M8 12h13M8 18h13M3 6h.01M3 12h.01M3 18h.01"/></svg></span><h2>The five tasks</h2></div>

  <div class="ttabs" role="tablist"><button class="ttab on" data-t="t1" role="tab" aria-selected="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M3 6h18v12H3z"/><path d="m3 7 9 6 9-6"/></svg>Connect inbox</button><button class="ttab" data-t="t2" role="tab" aria-selected="false"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M3 8.5V6a3 3 0 0 1 3-3h2.5"/><path d="M15.5 3H18a3 3 0 0 1 3 3v2.5"/><path d="M21 15.5V18a3 3 0 0 1-3 3h-2.5"/><path d="M8.5 21H6a3 3 0 0 1-3-3v-2.5"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3.2"/></svg>Capture a job</button><button class="ttab" data-t="t3" role="tab" aria-selected="false"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M6 3h9l4 4v14H6z"/><path d="M14 3v4h4"/><path d="M9.5 12h6M9.5 16h4"/></svg>Choose a resume</button><button class="ttab" data-t="t4" role="tab" aria-selected="false"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M18 8A6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 7-3 8-3 8h18s-3-1-3-8"/><path d="M10.5 21a2 2 0 0 0 3 0"/></svg>Trust an update</button><button class="ttab" data-t="t5" role="tab" aria-selected="false"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="16" rx="3"/><path d="M3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4"/></svg>Schedule clash</button></div>
  <div class="task show" id="t1">
    <div class="thead"><span class="tico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M3 6h18v12H3z"/><path d="m3 7 9 6 9-6"/></svg></span><div><p class="tnum">Task 1</p><h3>Connect your placement inbox</h3></div></div>
    <div class="quote">“Connect the email you actually use to apply for jobs.”</div>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">What I wanted to learn</span><span>Can users understand the email setup and move through onboarding without confusion?</span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Flow</span><span class="flow">
        <span class="chip">Welcome</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Choose inbox</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Gmail consent</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Connected</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Student Passport</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Home</span></span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Time</span><span>Use observed timings only. Completed flows varied; longer flows included extra Student Passport interaction.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="obs"><div class="h">What I observed</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Most users found Student Passport confusing.</li>
        <li>The previous screen did not clearly explain what Student Passport was or why it was needed.</li>
        <li>Many users skipped “Fill it in now · 2 min” because they wanted to explore the app first.</li>
        <li>4 users selected “I use more than one” for placement emails.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="out">
      <div class="o fr"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3 2 20h20z"/><path d="M12 10v4M12 17h.01"/></svg>Error / friction</span><span>Student Passport was unclear, and completing it felt like extra setup before users had seen the value of the product.</span></div>
      <div class="o ki"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9.2 16.5a6 6 0 1 1 5.6 0v2.2H9.2z"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg>Key insight</span><span>Users wanted to see the value of placED. before putting effort into setting it up.</span></div>
      <div class="o dc"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 20 16 8"/><path d="M14 4v4M12 6h4M18 12v3M16.5 13.5h3"/></svg>Design change</span><span>Move Student Passport away from the first setup step and introduce it when its benefit is clear.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="task" id="t2">
    <div class="thead"><span class="tico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M3 8.5V6a3 3 0 0 1 3-3h2.5"/><path d="M15.5 3H18a3 3 0 0 1 3 3v2.5"/><path d="M21 15.5V18a3 3 0 0 1-3 3h-2.5"/><path d="M8.5 21H6a3 3 0 0 1-3-3v-2.5"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3.2"/></svg></span><div><p class="tnum">Task 2</p><h3>Capture a job</h3></div></div>
    <div class="quote">“You’ve found a job application posting. Get it into the app.”</div>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">What I wanted to learn</span><span>Can users find and use the job-capture feature without help?</span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Flow</span><span class="flow">
        <span class="chip">Home</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">How capture works</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Chrome</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Search</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Job posting</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Capture</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Review</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Journey</span></span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Time</span><span>Use observed timings only. Users spent noticeable time in the capture explainer before reaching the job.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="obs"><div class="h">What I observed</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Users clicked “Capture this job” while still on the tutorial screen.</li>
        <li>This made the interaction confusing because the tutorial and the real action looked too closely related.</li>
        <li>The “How capture works” section contained too much text.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="out">
      <div class="o fr"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3 2 20h20z"/><path d="M12 10v4M12 17h.01"/></svg>Error / friction</span><span>Users treated the tutorial CTA as the real capture action, and the amount of text slowed understanding.</span></div>
      <div class="o ki"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9.2 16.5a6 6 0 1 1 5.6 0v2.2H9.2z"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg>Key insight</span><span>Users understood the action faster than they understood the explanation.</span></div>
      <div class="o dc"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 20 16 8"/><path d="M14 4v4M12 6h4M18 12v3M16.5 13.5h3"/></svg>Design change</span><span>Replace the text-heavy tutorial with a short visual: Open job → Tap placED. → Review &amp; save.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="task" id="t3">
    <div class="thead"><span class="tico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M6 3h9l4 4v14H6z"/><path d="M14 3v4h4"/><path d="M9.5 12h6M9.5 16h4"/></svg></span><div><p class="tnum">Task 3</p><h3>Choose a resume &amp; apply</h3></div></div>
    <div class="quote">“Open the job you’ve saved, choose which resume to send, apply on the portal, then make sure the app knows.”</div>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">What I wanted to learn</span><span>Can users choose the appropriate resume and understand how the application status is updated?</span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Flow</span><span class="flow">
        <span class="chip">Journey</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Job detail</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Resume choice</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Application portal</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Applied</span></span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Time</span><span>Use the recorded timing for each participant where a reliable endpoint exists.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="obs"><div class="h">What I observed</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Most users completed the main task without major difficulty.</li>
        <li>4 users skipped the Resume V5 checkbox.</li>
        <li>Most users who noticed V5 asked what “V5” meant.</li>
        <li>Some users explored the resume comparison screens.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="out">
      <div class="o fr"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3 2 20h20z"/><path d="M12 10v4M12 17h.01"/></svg>Error / friction</span><span>The resume version label was unfamiliar. Users understood the resume choice better than the version naming.</span></div>
      <div class="o ki"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9.2 16.5a6 6 0 1 1 5.6 0v2.2H9.2z"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg>Key insight</span><span>The recommendation was useful, but the system terminology was not immediately clear.</span></div>
      <div class="o dc"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 20 16 8"/><path d="M14 4v4M12 6h4M18 12v3M16.5 13.5h3"/></svg>Design change</span><span>Show the purpose first: “UX / Product Resume - V5 · Updated 2 Aug - 82% match.”</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="task" id="t4">
    <div class="thead"><span class="tico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M18 8A6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 7-3 8-3 8h18s-3-1-3-8"/><path d="M10.5 21a2 2 0 0 0 3 0"/></svg></span><div><p class="tnum">Task 4</p><h3>Trust an automatic update</h3></div></div>
    <div class="quote">“A notification says Adobe shortlisted you. Open it and decide whether you believe it.”</div>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">What I wanted to learn</span><span>Will users understand and verify an automatically detected status change?</span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Flow</span><span class="flow">
        <span class="chip">Notification</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Job detail</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Why this changed</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Confirm / Reject</span></span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Time</span><span>Use observed timings only; do not estimate missing endpoints.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="obs"><div class="h">What I observed</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Almost all 10 users skipped “How did we know? →”.</li>
        <li>Only 2 users opened the reasoning.</li>
        <li>Those 2 users were concerned about what would happen if they confirmed the status and later needed to change it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="out">
      <div class="o fr"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3 2 20h20z"/><path d="M12 10v4M12 17h.01"/></svg>Error / friction</span><span>Important trust information looked optional, and users were not sure whether an automatic status could be changed later.</span></div>
      <div class="o ki"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9.2 16.5a6 6 0 1 1 5.6 0v2.2H9.2z"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg>Key insight</span><span>Users do not necessarily reject automation. They want to understand it and remain in control.</span></div>
      <div class="o dc"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 20 16 8"/><path d="M14 4v4M12 6h4M18 12v3M16.5 13.5h3"/></svg>Design change</span><span>Show the source and reasoning directly in the confirmation flow, and clearly say the status can be changed later.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="task" id="t5">
    <div class="thead"><span class="tico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="16" rx="3"/><path d="M3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4"/></svg></span><div><p class="tnum">Task 5</p><h3>Resolve a schedule clash</h3></div></div>
    <div class="quote">“Two things clash on Friday morning. Sort it out.”</div>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">What I wanted to learn</span><span>Can users understand the conflict, choose what should move and review the generated communication?</span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Flow</span><span class="flow">
        <span class="chip">Home alert</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Calendar</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Choose which moves</span><span class="arw">→</span><span class="chip">Draft request</span><span class="arw">→</span>
        <span class="chip">Send</span></span></div>
      <div class="mrow"><span class="k">Time</span><span>Use observed timings only. Do not estimate missing endpoints.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="obs"><div class="h">What I observed</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Users understood the schedule clash.</li>
        <li>Users could identify the overlapping placement activities.</li>
        <li>Most users asked whether they could edit the generated email before sending it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="out">
      <div class="o fr"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3 2 20h20z"/><path d="M12 10v4M12 17h.01"/></svg>Error / friction</span><span>Users wanted control over the message before it was sent.</span></div>
      <div class="o ki"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9.2 16.5a6 6 0 1 1 5.6 0v2.2H9.2z"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg>Key insight</span><span>Users are comfortable letting placED. prepare the action, but they want the final say.</span></div>
      <div class="o dc"><span class="l"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 20 16 8"/><path d="M14 4v4M12 6h4M18 12v3M16.5 13.5h3"/></svg>Design change</span><span>Use Draft → Review → Edit → Send instead of sending immediately.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="cross">
  <div class="sh"><span class="tile"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><rect x="3" y="3" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="2"/><rect x="13.5" y="3" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="2"/><rect x="3" y="13.5" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="2"/><rect x="13.5" y="13.5" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="2"/></svg></span><h2>Cross-task findings</h2></div>
  <p>Looking across the five tasks, the observations point to four larger patterns.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <tr><th>Finding</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Design response</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Users want to explore before setup</td><td>Student Passport was often skipped.</td><td>Reduce early setup and introduce profile completion when useful.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Use language users understand</td><td>Users were confused by Student Passport and “V5”.</td><td>Explain the purpose first; keep system labels secondary.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Important information should not look optional</td><td>Almost all 10 users skipped “How did we know?”.</td><td>Bring important reasoning into the main interaction.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Automate the work, not the decision</td><td>Users wanted to change status and edit the email.</td><td>Use Suggest → Review → Confirm.</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="worked">
  <div class="sh"><span class="tile"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="m8.4 12.2 2.4 2.4 4.8-5"/></svg></span><h2>What worked</h2></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Most users could complete the main application flow.</li>
    <li>All of them agreed to give their email access to the application. (Important)</li>
    <li>Users understood the schedule clash.</li>
    <li>Email connection was generally understandable.</li>
    <li>Users were open to automatic status updates; their concern was mainly verification and control.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

